## Basic Størmer-Verlet Integration
*with haptic feedback feature*

In this example, we create a small widget with bouncing balls inside a disk. Each time a ball hits a wall, a short vibration is triggered on a connected haptic device.

:::warning
The haptic feature will most likely only work on macOS devices.
:::

Click to add more balls:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Basic Størmer-Verlet Integration
*with haptic feedback feature*

In this example, we create a small widget with bouncing balls inside a disk. Each time a ball hits a wall, a short vibration is triggered on a connected haptic device.

:::warning
The haptic feature will most likely only work on macOS devices.
:::

Click to add more balls:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Module[{balls1, balls2, balls3, handler},
  balls1 = balls2 = balls3 = {RandomReal[{-0.5,0.5}, 2]};
  balls2 = balls2 + {0.01 RandomReal[{-1,1}, 2]};
  
  handler = Function[Null,
    balls3 = balls2;
    balls2 = balls1;
    balls1 = Map[Function[p, 
      If[Norm[p] > 1,
        HapticFeedback[]; 
        Normalize[p] 0.99
      ,
        p
      ]
    ], (2 balls2 - balls3)];
  ];

  Graphics[{
    LightBlue, EventHandler[Disk[], {"click" -> Function[xy,
      AppendTo[balls1, xy];
      AppendTo[balls2, xy + 0.01 RandomReal[{-1,1}, 2]];
      AppendTo[balls3, xy];
    ]}], Black, PointSize[0.08], Point[balls1 // Offload],
    EventHandler[AnimationFrameListener[balls1 // Offload], handler]
  }, "Controls"->False, "TransitionType"->None
  ]
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Physics Behind
Here we rely on the Verlet integration method, which operates only in coordinate space (no velocities or accelerations need to be stored):

$$
x_{i+1} = 2x_i - x_{i-1} + a_i\, \delta t^2
$$

In our case, we have $a_i = 0$, and only boundary conditions are applied afterward, i.e., this part:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
